.docname {Objects}
.include {docs}

How vCortex turns the dots that are **not** wall into boxes: a desk, a wardrobe, a bin. Every
object is a box seen from above, with a bottom and a top.

## What comes in, what goes out

| | |
|---|---|
| **In** | The **furniture dots**: dots in the height band, inside a room outline, farther than 10 cm from every wall (see step B5 of [Walls](walls.qd)). The floor plane. The object settings |
| **Out** | A list of objects: centre, size, bottom, top, number of dots |
| **Code** | `model/object/` (4 files), called from `model/model.cpp` |

The step works on the **squared map** (the walls run along x and y), so every box is aligned with
the walls. It is turned back to the map's own angle at the end.

.mermaid caption:{The eight steps from dots to objects.}
    flowchart TD
        P[Furniture dots] --> G[O1 grid:<br/>count, top, occupied]
        G --> C[O2 closing:<br/>fill gaps inside]
        C --> O[O3 opening:<br/>cut thin bridges = cores]
        O --> L[O4 label the cores]
        L --> R[O5 give the cut cells back]
        R --> H[O6 split by height<br/>optional, off by default]
        H --> B[O7 cut each piece into boxes]
        B --> M[O8 make the objects]

## Words and sizes

| Word | Meaning | Default |
|---|---|---|
| **Cell** | A square of the top view | 5 cm |
| **Occupied** | A cell with at least `cell_min_points` dots | 1 dot |
| **Piece** | A group of occupied cells that belong to one thing | |
| **Radius** | In cells: 10 cm ÷ 5 cm = **2 cells**. The window it makes is 5 cells wide | |
| **Grow** | Mark every cell that has a marked cell within the radius | |
| **Shrink** | Keep only the cells whose whole window is marked | |

## O1. The grid

**Code:** [`grid.cpp:14`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/grid.cpp#L14) `build_object_grid`

- A top view, 5 cm cells, over the furniture dots, plus a **margin** of 3 cells (15 cm) on every
  side. The margin is the widest radius (2) plus one empty cell, so the steps below never run off
  the edge.
- For each cell: how many dots (`point_count`), and the **highest** one, as a height above the
  floor plane (`top_metres`).
- A cell is **occupied** when it holds at least `cell_min_points` dots (default 1).
- Each dot remembers its cell, so a piece can be mapped back to its dots later.

## O2. Closing: fill the gaps inside an object

**Code:** [`grid.cpp:61`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/grid.cpp#L61) `grow_by_cell` · [`grid.cpp:75`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/grid.cpp#L75) `shrink_by_cell` · [`objects.cpp:38`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/objects.cpp#L38) `closing`

**Grow** by `close_radius`, then **shrink** by the same. Gaps inside a thing, left by a glass top
or a missed return, get filled. The outside edge stays where it was.

- A gap **4 cells (20 cm) wide or less** is filled. A gap of 5 cells (25 cm) is not.
- That is the rule "gaps up to twice `close_radius` fill in".

## O3. Opening: cut the thin bridges between two objects

**Code:** [`objects.cpp:40`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/objects.cpp#L40) `opening`

On the closed picture, **shrink** by `split_radius`, then **grow** by the same. A part that is
**4 cells (20 cm) wide or less** disappears; a part 5 cells (25 cm) or more comes back as it was.
What is left are the **cores**: the solid bodies of the objects.

**Example.** Two blocks of 8 × 8 cells, a thin bridge between them, and a 2 × 2 hole in the left
block. A `#` is an occupied cell. (I ran the same rules on this small picture.)

![Closing, opening and labelling on a small example](images/objects-morphology.svg "Steps O1 to O5 on two blocks with a thin bridge.")

- Closing fills the hole of the left block, and the bridge keeps its 3 rows.
- Opening removes the bridge (3 cells wide, 4 or less): two cores are left.

## O4. Label the cores

**Code:** [`labels.cpp:15`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/labels.cpp#L15) `label_objects`

Each group of cores joined by their 4 sides gets its own number, counting from 1. 0 means "no
piece".

## O5. Give the cut cells back

**Code:** [`labels.cpp:37`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/labels.cpp#L37) `assign_non_labelled_objects`

Opening cut away cells that belong to an object: the bridge above, the thin edges. Now every label
**spreads** over the closed picture, all labels at the same speed, so a cell joins the label
**fewest steps away**. It never leaves the closed region.

- The bridge above is split **halfway**: its left half joins the left block, its right half the
  right block.
- A thin thing that never had a core (a leg, a pole) forms a region no label reaches. It gets a
  **new label**, so it stays a piece of its own.

## O6. Split by height (off by default)

**Code:** [`labels.cpp:219`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/labels.cpp#L219) `split_by_height` · [`labels.cpp:131`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/labels.cpp#L131) `grow_height_regions` · [`labels.cpp:197`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/labels.cpp#L197) `merge_small_regions` · [`objects.cpp:44`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/objects.cpp#L44) `only if > 0`

With `height_step` > 0, each piece is cut where the tops of two neighbouring cells differ by more
than `height_step`. A cupboard on top of a desk would become two pieces.

- Cells joined by tops that differ by at most `height_step` form a **region**.
- A region of fewer than `min_piece_cells` = 8 cells is mostly noise: it joins the neighbour
  region with the closest mean top.
- Cells that closing filled have no top, so they join the nearest region.

The default `height_step` is **0: this step does nothing**.

## O7. Cut each piece into boxes

**Code:** [`boxes.cpp:63`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/boxes.cpp#L63) `split_into_boxes` · [`boxes.cpp:22`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/boxes.cpp#L22) `find_largest_full_rectangle` · [`boxes.cpp:57`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/boxes.cpp#L57) `is_too_thin`

A piece is not always a rectangle: an L, a U, a ring. Each piece is cut into boxes.

1. Find the **largest full rectangle** inside the piece (by area, in cells).
2. If both its sides are at least `min_box_side` = 20 cm (4 cells), it becomes a box; its cells are
   taken out of the piece. Go back to 1.
3. Stop at the first rectangle that is too thin.
4. The cells left over (the steps of a ragged edge) join the **nearest box of their own piece**.
5. A piece too thin for any box keeps one box: the bounding box of its cells.

The largest rectangle is found row by row: for each column, count how many occupied cells are
stacked up to this row; a stack of rising heights closes, at each drop, the widest rectangle of the
height that ends.

**Real test cases** (sizes in cells, 5 cm each; the tests use a minimum side of 2 cells):

| Shape | Boxes |
|---|---|
| A rectangle 8 × 4 with a one-cell step on one side | **1** box 8 × 4; the step joins it |
| An L: 2-wide column of 3 on a 8 × 2 base | **2**: 8 × 2 and 2 × 3 |
| A U: two 2 × 3 arms on an 8 × 2 base | **3**: 8 × 2, 2 × 3, 2 × 3 |
| A ring, 10 × 10 with a 6 × 6 hole | **4** boxes; the hole stays empty |
| Columns of 1, 2, 3 cells | The 2 × 2 goes first (area 4), not the 1 × 3 column or the 3 × 1 row |

So one **L-shaped** desk gives **two objects**.

![Test shapes cut into boxes](images/objects-boxes.svg "The largest rectangle goes first.")

## O8. Make the objects

**Code:** [`objects.cpp:20`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/objects.cpp#L20) `find_objects` · [`objects.cpp:69`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/objects.cpp#L69) `drop rule` · [`objects.cpp:83`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/object/objects.cpp#L83) `bottom and top` · [`model.cpp:110`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/model.cpp#L110) `yaw`

For each box:

1. Collect the dots whose cell is in the box.
2. **Drop it** when its piece holds fewer than `cluster_min_points` = 20 dots (noise), or when the
   box holds no dot at all (closing filled it).
3. **Size:** from the cells, so a multiple of 5 cm. The far corner is one cell past the last cell.
4. **Bottom and top:** `bottom_z` and `top_z_seen` are the **z of the lowest and the highest dot**
   in the box. Raw z in the map, not above the floor.
5. `num_points` is the number of dots.

Then all objects are sorted by centre (x first, then y) and numbered `object-1`, `object-2`, …

Back in the model (see [Rooms](rooms.qd), B5): the centre is turned back to the map's angle, the
box's `yaw` is set to that angle, and the object gets a `room_id` when its centre lies inside a
closed room.

## Real numbers

The room of the [Overview](introduction.qd): 19 objects, from 71,262 dots. Sizes in metres:

| Object | Size | Dots | Top seen, above `floor_z` |
|---|---|---|---|
| `object-15` (most dots) | 4.10 × 0.95 | 11,909 | 1.79 m |
| `object-2` | 1.25 × 2.45 | 10,561 | 1.83 m |
| `object-3` | 3.70 × 1.05 | 9,205 | 1.39 m |
| `object-13` (fewest dots) | 1.05 × 0.30 | 394 | 0.83 m |
| `object-10` (thinnest) | 0.20 × 1.35 | 700 | 0.93 m |

- Every size is a multiple of 0.05 m: cells.
- The thinnest side is 0.20 m: `min_box_side`.
- All 19 objects have the **same `yaw`**, 0.2932 rad = 16.8°: the angle of the map. A box is
  never turned against the walls.
- All 19 are in `room-1`.

![The 19 objects of the real room](images/objects-real-room.svg "Real data: boxes in the squared map.")

**Why the bottom looks too high.** `object-1`: `bottom_z` = −0.305, `floor_z` = −0.455, so the
bottom seems 0.15 m above the floor. But no dot of the band lies lower than **0.30 m** above the
floor, so the true bottom is at least that. The gap is the **tilt**: the floor leans by 2.4°, which
is 4.1 cm per metre, and `object-1` stands 3.6 m from the middle of the walls, where `floor_z` is
measured: 3.6 × 0.041 = 0.15 m. Read `bottom_z` and `top_z_seen` as heights in the map, and do not
subtract `floor_z` blindly.

![Floor tilt and the bottom of object-1](images/objects-tilt.svg "Why bottom_z − floor_z is not the height above the floor.")

## The settings of this chapter

| Setting | Default | Used in |
|---|---|---|
| `objects.cell` | 0.05 m | O1 |
| `objects.cell_min_points` | 1 | O1 |
| `objects.close_radius` | 0.10 m | O2 |
| `objects.split_radius` | 0.10 m | O3 |
| `objects.height_step` | 0 (off) | O6 |
| `objects.min_piece_cells` | 8 | O6 |
| `objects.min_box_side` | 0.20 m | O7 |
| `objects.cluster_min_points` | 20 | O8 |
| `walls.object_clearance` | 0.10 m | which dots are furniture |
| `candidates.band_lo` | 0.30 m | the lowest dot that can be furniture |

Explained in [Settings](settings.qd).

