## What the tests check

Six cases in `boxes_test`, listed in O7. The other steps (O1 to O6, O8) have **no test**.

## Gaps found

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| `bottom_z` and `top_z_seen` are raw map heights, not heights above the floor. On a tilted floor they are off by about 4 cm per metre away from the middle of the walls (15 cm at 3.6 m in the real run) | `objects.cpp` |
| **Nothing under 30 cm is an object.** The dots start at `candidates.band_lo` above the floor, so a box on the floor or a low shelf is never seen | `walls.cpp` |
| Every box is aligned with the walls, so a desk turned 30° gets a box that is too big. All objects share the map's `yaw` | `objects.cpp` |
| An L or a U gives several objects, not one | `boxes.cpp` |
| The object has no kind: it is always `"unknown"` | `objects.cpp` |
| The scan count of a dot (`num_scan_seen`) is not used here, so a person who walked past the robot can leave a small object | `objects.cpp` |
| O6 (split by height) is off by default and has no test | `labels.cpp` |
| The comment in `boxes.cpp` notes that the search for the largest rectangle scans the whole grid for every box; it may slow down when there are many pieces | `boxes.cpp` |
| O1 to O6 and O8 have no test | `backend/tests/` |
